---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { SITE } from '../data/site';
---
<Base path="/fulfillment" title="Fulfillment"
      description="How Austin Devs engagements are scoped, delivered, billed, and ended.">
  <section class="panel" data-route="/fulfillment" data-current>
    <div class="inner prose">
      <h1 class="page-title">Fulfillment</h1>
      <p class="lede">What you are buying, how it gets delivered, and how it ends. Written plainly rather than as boilerplate.</p>

      <h2>What is sold</h2>
      <p>
        {SITE.legal} sells professional services: technology strategy, engineering leadership, software
        development, and the related advisory work described on the services page. Nothing here is a
        physical product, and there is no shipping involved. Deliverables are things like working software,
        source code in your own repositories, architecture and diligence documents, and time in meetings.
      </p>

      <h2>How an engagement starts</h2>
      <p>
        Every engagement starts with a conversation, not an order form. Scope, rate, and cadence are agreed
        in writing first, whether that is a statement of work, a consulting agreement, or an email that both
        sides confirm. Work does not begin before that exists.
      </p>

      <h2>Shapes of engagement</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Fractional CTO.</strong> A recurring monthly commitment for a set number of days or hours, billed monthly.</li>
        <li><strong>Project work.</strong> A defined deliverable with a fixed fee or an estimated range, billed at agreed milestones.</li>
        <li><strong>Advisory and diligence.</strong> Short bounded engagements, usually billed on completion.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Delivery</h2>
      <p>
        Code is delivered into your repositories and your infrastructure as it is written, not held back until
        the end. Documents are delivered in editable formats you keep. Timelines are agreed per engagement and
        kept current in writing when they change, which on real projects they sometimes do.
      </p>

      <h2>Billing</h2>
      <p>
        Invoices are issued electronically and are due on the terms stated on the invoice, ordinarily net 15.
        Recurring engagements are billed monthly in advance unless agreed otherwise. Applicable taxes are added
        where required.
      </p>

      <h2>Changes, pausing, and cancellation</h2>
      <p>
        Recurring engagements can be ended by either side with 30 days written notice. Project work can be
        stopped at any point; you are billed for work completed to that date and you keep everything produced
        up to it. Scope changes are agreed in writing before the work that depends on them starts.
      </p>

      <h2>Refunds</h2>
      <p>
        Fees for work already performed are not refundable, because the work has been delivered and is yours to
        keep. Amounts paid in advance for work not yet performed are refunded in full when an engagement ends.
        If something delivered is not what was agreed, the first remedy is to put it right; raise it and it gets
        addressed.
      </p>

      <h2>Ownership</h2>
      <p>
        On payment, work product created specifically for you is yours. Pre-existing tools, libraries, and general
        know-how brought to the engagement remain owned by {SITE.legal}, licensed to you for use in what was
        delivered. Anything more specific is set out in the engagement agreement, which takes precedence over
        this page.
      </p>

      <h2>Questions</h2>
      <p>Email <a href={`mailto:${SITE.email}`}>{SITE.email}</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
